use @piton/belay

from ../FactoryDashboard import FactoryDashboard

export skill ImplementFactoryDashboard:
    description:
        Inspects an application's build orchestration, asks focused setup
        questions, and implements a monitoring dashboard.
    useWhen:
        asked to implement or integrate the orchestrated build dashboard
        in a consuming application
    prompt:
        Implement the dashboard in the current consuming application using
        @{FactoryDashboard} as a general lattice to adapt to this
        application's workflow, not as a drop-in blueprint to reproduce
        unchanged, under its drop-in contract: assume only stable run,
        task, agent, and attempt identity and lifecycle from the host,
        keep the core dashboard read-only and failure-isolated, and treat
        every other capability as discovered, opportunistic enrichment.
        Build the parts that make sense here, take the rest as guidance
        for building the equivalent thing in this workflow, and preserve
        each requirement's intent when its form changes. Start by
        inspecting the application and asking focused questions about its
        build setup. After the necessary answers, implement and verify the
        dashboard; do not stop at a specification or plan.

    adaptToThisWorkflow:
        Every consuming workflow differs in orchestration, harnesses,
        telemetry, limits, and interface conventions. Decide from the
        inspected application which capabilities, sources, sections, and
        defaults in @{FactoryDashboard} apply, which need a local
        equivalent, and which do not apply at all. Do not force a source,
        section, breakdown, or presentation form that this workflow
        cannot support or does not need; omit or defer it instead. Keep
        the firm invariants in @{FactoryDashboard}: never present
        fabricated or unavailable values as observed telemetry, do not
        double-count combined work or usage, and keep the dashboard
        observational. Record each material adaptation or omission, and
        its reason, in the consuming specification.

    inspectTheApplication:
        Before Piton work, run piton agent --print-fluency in the consuming
        project and read the complete output. If it fails, report the
        failure instead of guessing Piton conventions. Read piton.config.pi,
        the entry file, relevant source specifications and compiled
        references, and applicable project guidance. Locate the application
        UI, build orchestration, harness integrations, and existing telemetry.
        Follow references only as needed.

        First establish the host's stable run, task, agent, and attempt
        identities and lifecycle signals, and how nested, parallel, and
        retried work maps into that canonical model; this mapping is the
        foundation the read-only core is built on, and work that cannot
        be mapped stays visible as its own row with unknown fields.

        Discover whether the consuming pipeline uses local or remote
        software builds, including mixed execution. Inspect its existing
        build tools and services for usable job telemetry; do not assume
        an agent's location is the location of its build jobs.

        Inspect available harness interfaces, event streams, logs, and
        temporary-file producers to establish what data actually exists.
        Use installed interfaces or authoritative documentation to verify
        capabilities; do not invent harness APIs or assume temporary files
        share a schema. Treat the package's candidate sources as possibilities.

    askFocusedQuestions:
        Ask exactly one question per turn and wait for the answer. Start
        with the most consequential missing information about the current
        orchestration setup. Do not ask for facts already established by
        the user, application, or specification. Use findings from inspection
        to offer concrete options and explain relevant limitations.

        Resolve only decisions needed for this implementation, such as
        which builds and harnesses to monitor, where to place the dashboard,
        which exposed usage limits matter, and whether missing telemetry
        can be supplied by an agreed integration. Choose each question from
        what remains unknown; do not turn these topics into a questionnaire.
        There is no fixed question budget. Stop questioning when there is
        enough information to implement, respecting any budget the user gives.

        If the user does not know an answer, investigate what is feasible
        and recommend an approach grounded in the application. Preserve
        unresolved values as unknown. Do not require a separate approval
        round before implementation when the user's request already
        authorizes it. If essential access or information is missing,
        identify the specific blocker and complete independent work.

    alignTheSpecification:
        Record confirmed integration decisions in the consuming project's
        own .pi source, using its established structure. Record what was
        taken from @{FactoryDashboard} as-is, what was adapted to this
        workflow, and what was omitted or deferred and why, so the local
        specification reads as this application's own design rather than
        a copy of the package. Preserve unrelated requirements and
        distinguish confirmed decisions from unresolved questions. Treat
        tethered package files as vendored dependencies; do not edit them
        to customize the consuming application. Keep lasting guidance
        changes in source and rebuild generated artifacts.

    implementTheDashboard:
        Use the application's existing stack and UI conventions. Build
        the read-only core first: from identity and lifecycle alone it
        must show what work is running, how it is nested and grouped,
        its status, and its running time, without requiring any change
        to the host. Keep that core failure-isolated, so a broken or
        slow source, adapter, or dashboard component degrades to stale
        or unknown on the dashboard and never delays, alters, or stops
        agent work, builds, or task execution. Where they fit this
        workflow, add progress and completion estimates, usage limits,
        and projections for reaching those limits as enrichment,
        following @{FactoryDashboard} as guidance adapted to the
        sources and workflow actually found; do not add a capability in
        a form this application cannot support.

        Connect to the verified sources. Preserve task, agent, harness,
        and run identity when combining data, and account for parallel
        execution when estimating completion. Avoid counting the same usage
        more than once when multiple sources report it. Represent the scope
        and reset period of a limit when the source supplies them.

        Clearly distinguish observed values, estimates, unknown values,
        and stale or disconnected sources. Do not present fabricated
        percentages, unavailable limits, or sample data as live telemetry.
        Ground projections in available observations and explain their
        basis where useful. Leave them unknown when data is insufficient.

        Implement the shared agent reporting, automatic update, source
        reconciliation, and freshness requirements in @{FactoryDashboard}.
        Define the concrete shared format and transport in the consuming
        specification and integrate reporting into its agent guidance or
        telemetry producers. Do not require complete harness metrics before
        supporting a source. Verify agent precedence when reports conflict
        and retain unknown values when no usable measurement exists.

        Keep the dashboard observational. Do not add pause, cancellation,
        or reassignment controls. If additional telemetry instrumentation
        is needed, fit it to the agreed integration without changing build
        scheduling or task execution behavior.

        When software builds are used, implement the buildExecution
        requirements in @{FactoryDashboard}. Reuse the application's
        existing build sources, associate jobs with agent tasks, and
        display local and remote execution with available status, timing,
        estimates, and existing detail or log links. Record unsupported
        telemetry as unknown rather than introducing a different build system.

    reportingLifecycleAndUsage:
        Where this workflow reports in the same way, implement the attempt
        identity, sequence ordering, reporter health, metric freshness,
        and completed-result rules in @{FactoryDashboard}; where its
        reporting lifecycle differs, build the local equivalent that
        keeps the same protections against stale, duplicated, or
        out-of-order data.
        Use a harness hook or background reporter for heartbeats while an
        agent is occupied. Implement usageReporting with available totals,
        trends, breakdowns, shared quotas, reset times, remaining capacity,
        exhaustion projections, and costs. Verify that replayed reports and
        overlapping cumulative totals and increments do not duplicate usage.
        Give every agent an exact progress-reporting command in its task
        instructions, implement usage history from harness-local records,
        quota projections and per-hour consumption rates, cost estimates
        from published rates with their source, phantom-build detection and
        a periodic stale-work sweep, following @{FactoryDashboard}.

    presentation:
        Treat the presentation guidance in @{FactoryDashboard} as a
        default lattice — a summary strip, sections with one story each,
        full data tables with header tooltips, page-only scrolling with
        stacked cards on narrow screens, concise explanations, and one
        consistent visual grammar — adapted to this application's design
        system, layout constraints, and the data it actually has, rather
        than reproduced element for element. Preserve its intent: a
        viewer can find running work, builds, quotas, and costs quickly,
        and observed and estimated values stay distinguishable. Verify
        the result with screenshots at desktop and phone widths in light
        and dark modes, using a headless browser when no interactive
        browser is available.

    verifyAndReport:
        Run piton format, piton check, and piton build after specification
        changes, fix reported source problems, and inspect generated diffs.
        Run the application's relevant checks and exercise the dashboard
        against available real telemetry or clearly identified fixtures.
        Verify that the core task view works from identity and lifecycle
        alone with every enrichment source absent, that unmapped and
        nested work stays visible, and that a failing or slow source
        degrades to stale or unknown without affecting host work. Verify
        concurrent task grouping and timing, usage calculations,
        estimate labeling, and missing or stale data behavior where relevant.
        Use browser verification when available for the implemented UI.

        Report what was implemented, what was adapted or omitted from
        @{FactoryDashboard} for this workflow and why, the connected data
        sources, checks actually run and their results, how to open the
        dashboard, and any remaining unsupported metrics or blocked
        integrations. Do not claim live integration was verified when
        only fixtures were available.
